"margin-top:3px;margin-right:5px;" src="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/photo/133401516.cms" /&gt;<![CDATA[]]></description><link><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/uk/man-dressed-as-grim-reaper-climbs-onto-hospital-roof-with-long-blade-apparently-trying-to-scare-patients/articleshow/133401516.cms]]></link><guid><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/uk/man-dressed-as-grim-reaper-climbs-onto-hospital-roof-with-long-blade-apparently-trying-to-scare-patients/articleshow/133401516.cms]]></guid><pubDate>2026-08-21T16:09:24+05:30</pubDate><dc:creator xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/">TOI World Desk</dc:creator><enclosure type="image/jpeg" url="https://static.toiimg.com/photo/msid-133401708,imgsize-1127607.cms"/></item><item><title><![CDATA[A 1,256-megawatt nuclear reactor on Lake Erie accidentally became a wildlife sanctuary, now spotted turtles nest inside its buffer zone]]></title><description>&lt;img border="0" hspace="10" align="left" style="margin-top:3px;margin-right:5px;" src="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/photo/133401446.cms" /&gt;<![CDATA[Over the last two centuries, Ohio has witnessed a drastic decline in wetlands, significantly affecting wildlife habitats. However, the land preserved around a nuclear power plant serves as an essential sanctuary for endangered spotted turtles. These turtles thrive in shallow waters rich in vegetation, and the plant's ongoing operations offer a critical generational opportunity for their survival. This unexpected refuge demonstrates nature's remarkable ability to endure amidst human expansion.]]></description><link><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/us/a-1256-megawatt-nuclear-reactor-on-lake-erie-accidentally-became-a-wildlife-sanctuary-now-spotted-turtles-nest-inside-its-buffer-zone/articleshow/133401446.cms]]></link><guid><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/us/a-1256-megawatt-nuclear-reactor-on-lake-erie-accidentally-became-a-wildlife-sanctuary-now-spotted-turtles-nest-inside-its-buffer-zone/articleshow/133401446.cms]]></guid><pubDate>2026-08-21T17:07:17+05:30</pubDate><dc:creator xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/">TOI World Desk</dc:creator><enclosure type="image/jpeg" url="https://static.toiimg.com/photo/msid-133401698,imgsize-132602.cms"/></item><item><title><![CDATA[In 1908, Eliza Homans donated 140 acres of land to stop future development, six years later it became a part of Acadia National Park]]></title><description>&lt;img border="0" hspace="10" align="left" style="margin-top:3px;margin-right:5px;" src="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/photo/133397892.cms" /&gt;<![CDATA[Eliza Homans made a remarkable contribution of 140 acres to establish Acadia National Park, incorporating breathtaking locations like the Bowl and the Beehive. This generous act marked the first significant donation to the Hancock County Trustees of Public Reservation, laying the groundwork for the park's creation. In recognition of her impact, Homans Path was later integrated into the park's expansive trail system, ensuring her legacy persists within its natural beauty.]]></description><link><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/us/in-1908-a-boston-woman-donated-140-acres-around-maines-beehive-and-bowl-to-stop-future-development-six-years-later-5000-acres-had-been-protected-for-what-became-acadia/articleshow/133397892.cms]]></link><guid><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/us/in-1908-a-boston-woman-donated-140-acres-around-maines-beehive-and-bowl-to-stop-future-development-six-years-later-5000-acres-had-been-protected-for-what-became-acadia/articleshow/133397892.cms]]></guid><pubDate>2026-08-21T16:00:00+05:30</pubDate><dc:creator xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/">TOI World Desk</dc:creator><enclosure type="image/jpeg" url="https://static.toiimg.com/photo/msid-133397988,imgsize-59144.cms"/></item></channel></rss>